  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nCould the Panhandles truly see a pattern shift next week to provide \nmore rain opportunities and heat relief? Several global models \ncontinue to suggest that the answer is yes, but don't get your \nhopes up for a widespread gullywasher or true \"cool down\". The \nwork week will start off solidly dominated by high pressure, with \ntriple digit heat holding steady. But long range data does show \ntroughing over the Great Lakes region sagging southward by \nmidweek, which would suppress high pressure further southwest over\nEl Paso. This synoptic pattern would allow northwest flow aloft \nto provide better disturbances and weak fronts for the region, \nespecially for the northern combined Panhandles. Ensemble members \nmostly agree this would increase rain chances each evening- \novernight Tue-Thu, but despite favorable deep layer moisture \n(PWATs 1.0-1.5\"), most members suggest rainfall totals wouldn't be\nterribly impressive (mostly 0.50\" or less). Temperatures are \nprogged to decline to the 90s Wed-Thu, but not for long as \nwidespread triple digits are favored to resume through the end of \nthe week. In summary, we have reason to be hopeful for better\nweather next week, but the expectation is that most locations are\nmore likely to maintain relatively hot and dry conditions.\n\nHarrel",
